If a run has length $L$, it contributes $L-1$ to the count of identical pairs.
We need total HH = 1 and TT = 1.
Suppose an H run has length 3. It contributes 2 HH. Too many.
So no run can have length $\ge 3$.
Thus, all runs must have length 1 or 2.
The contribution to HH count is the number of runs of H with length 2.
Let $n_{H,2}$ be the number of H runs of length 2. Then $n_{H,2} = 1$.
Similarly $n_{T,2} = 1$.
All other H runs must be length 1. All other T runs must be length 1.
Total length = $\sum L_i = (\text{#H runs} \times 1) + n_{H,2} \times 1 + (1 \times (2-1)) = \text{Total count of H runs} + 1$.
