LM Response: [[ ## reasoning ## ]]
The problem asks for the probability that some student has to pass by another student to get to an aisle. The seats are arranged in a row with aisles only on the two sides (left and right ends). Let the seats be $S_1, S_2, \dots, S_6$. Students sit in these seats.
The condition "pass by another student to get to an aisle" implies that a student sitting in an interior seat (not at an end) cannot reach an aisle without walking past another student because aisles are only at the ends. Wait, let's re-read carefully.
"aisles only on the two sides of the row". This usually means aisle is at position 0 and position 7 (if seats are 1..6).
If a student is in seat $k$ ($1 < k < 6$), to get to an aisle, they must walk left to seat 1 then out, or right to seat 6 then out.
Path left: $k \to k-1 \to \dots \to 1 \to \text{aisle}$. They pass students in $k-1, \dots, 1$.
Path right: $k \to k+1 \to \dots \to 6 \to \text{aisle}$. They pass students in $k+1, \dots, 6$.
The question asks: "what is the probability that some student will have to pass by another student to get to an aisle?"
