{"code": "from sympy import symbols, pi, integrate, sqrt\n\n# Based on the derivation: r = 1 / (2 * cos(alpha))\n# Since A(A) is inside the unit circle, r < 1.\n# 1 / (2 * cos(alpha)) < 1 => cos(alpha) > 1/2.\n# For a valid non-degenerate triangle OAB, alpha != 0, so cos(alpha) < 1.\n# Thus 1/2 < cos(alpha) < 1.\n# This implies 1/2 < r < 1.\n# The region of points A is the annulus 1/2 < |x| < 1.\n# Area = pi * (1^2 - (1/2)^2) = 3pi/4.\n\narea = pi * (1**2 - (1/2)**2)\nprint(f\"Area: {area}\")\nprint(f\"Numeric value: {float(area)}\")\n\n# Let's also double check if there are any constraints on alpha that prevent ANY solution for some r.\n# We established r = 1/(2 cos(alpha)).\n# For a fixed r, we need cos(alpha) = 1/(2r).\n# Since cos(alpha) can take any value in (-1, 1) except maybe specific points depending on geometry constraints...\n# The only constraint is that O, A, B are not collinear.\n# If alpha is close to 0 or pi, cos is close to +/- 1.\n# r is close to 1/2 or -1/2. Since r > 0, close to 1/2.\n# As alpha -> 0, r -> 1/2.\n# Is there any r in (1/2, 1) for which no valid alpha exists?\n# We just need cos(alpha) = 1/(2r). Since 1/(2r) is in (1/2, 1) for r in (1/2, 1), a valid alpha always exists.\n# The only edge case is alpha=0 which gives collinear points.\n# But for any fixed r > 1/2, 1/(2r) < 1, so cos(alpha) < 1, so alpha != 0.\n# So all r in (1/2, 1) are valid.\n\nprint(\"Verification passed: Range (1/2, 1) is valid.\")"}

