--- Tool Creator Reasoning ---
To solve the integral $I = \int_{0}^{\pi} \frac{2 \sin \theta+3 \cos \theta-3}{13 \cos \theta-5} \mathrm{d} \theta$, we can split the numerator to relate it to the denominator.
Let $u = 13 \cos \theta - 5$. Then $du = -13 \sin \theta \, d\theta$, so $\sin \theta \, d\theta = -du/13$.
We can rewrite the numerator $2 \sin \theta + 3 \cos \theta - 3$ as a combination of terms involving the denominator and a constant, plus a term with $\sin \theta$.
Consider the ansatz: $2 \sin \theta + 3 \cos \theta - 3 = A (13 \cos \theta - 5) + B \sin \theta + C$.
Matching coefficients:
Constant term: $-5A + C = -3$
$\cos \theta$ term: $13A = 3 \implies A = 3/13$
$\sin \theta$ term: $B = 2$
Then $C = -3 + 5A = -3 + 15/13 = (-39+15)/13 = -24/13$.
So the integrand is:
