Using the generating function for the difference $D = X - Y$, the probability $P(X-Y=k)$ is given by the coefficient of $x^k$ in the expansion of the generating function for $X$ multiplied by the generating function for $-Y$. Specifically, $P(X=k) = \binom{N}{k}(1/2)^N$ and $P(Y=j) = \binom{N}{j}(1/2)^N$.
$$ P(X-Y=1) = \sum_{k=1}^N P(X=k)P(Y=k-1) = \sum_{k=1}^N \binom{N}{k}\binom{N}{k-1} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{2N} $$
This sum corresponds to the coefficient of $x^{N+1}$ (or $x^{N-1}$ via symmetry) in the polynomial $(1+x)^{2N}$. Specifically, it is known that $\sum_k \binom{N}{k}\binom{N}{k-1} = \binom{2N}{N+1}$.
Thus, the probability is:
$$ P(X=Y+1) = \binom{2N}{N+1} \left(\frac{1}{4}\right)^N = \frac{\binom{4030}{2016}}{4^{2015}} $$
