\(10^{x+1} = 10^{y-1} + 1\)
\(10 \cdot 10^x = \frac{10^y}{10} + 1\)
\(100 \cdot 10^x = 10^y + 10\)
\(10^y = 100 \cdot 10^x - 10\)

From equation (2):
\(y + 1 = \log_{10}(10^{x}+1)\)
\(10^{y+1} = 10^x + 1\)
\(10 \cdot 10^y = 10^x + 1\)

Now substitute the expression for \(10^y\) from the first derived equation into the second:
\(10 \cdot (100 \cdot 10^x - 10) = 10^x + 1\)
\(1000 \cdot 10^x - 100 = 10^x + 1\)
\(999 \cdot 10^x = 101\)
\(10^x = \frac{101}{999}\)

Now find \(10^y\):
\(10^y = 10 \cdot 10^y / 10 = 100 \cdot 10^x - 10\)
\(10^y = 100 \cdot \frac{101}{999} - 10\)
\(10^y = \frac{10100}{999} - \frac{9990}{999}\)
\(10^y = \frac{110}{999}\)

The problem asks for \(10^{x-y}\).
