We have 4 people, so there are $10^4$ total equally likely outcomes.

Let $A$ be the event that there exists some number $k \in S$ such that $k$ is not selected by any of the 4 people.
We want to calculate $P(A)$.

It is often easier to count the complement event $A^c$: every number in $S$ is selected by at least one person.
If every number is selected, then the union of the 4 chosen sets must be $S$.
Each chosen set has size 5. The union of 4 sets of size 5 in a set of size 10 can cover the entire set.

Let the choices be $C_1, C_2, C_3, C_4$. Each $C_i$ is an interval of length 5 

