Let the blocks be indexed $1, 2, \dots, 10$.
There are $B = \{1, 2, \dots, 10\}$ for boys and $G = \{1, 2, \dots, 10\}$ for girls.
We need to find the number of permutations $\sigma$ of $\{1, \dots, 10\}$ such that for every $i$, $|i - \sigma(i)| \le 1$.

This is a standard problem in combinatorics often referred to as counting "restricted permutations" or "permutations with restricted displacement". A permutation satisfying $|i - \sigma(i)| \le 1$ for all $i$ is composed only of fixed points ($i \to i$) and transpositions of adjacent elements ($i \leftrightarrow i+1$). None of these form cycles of length greater than 2, and specifically, there are no cycles like $(1 \to 2 \to 1)$ which is just a transposition, but also no cycles like $(1 \to 2 \to 3 \to 1)$ because $3 \to 1$ would require $|3-1|=2 > 1$.
Essentially, for each $i$, $\sigma(i)$ can be $i-1$, $i$, or $i+1$.
