Condition 2 states maximality. This applies to adding girls or adding boys.
- If we can add a girl $g' \in F \setminus G$, it must not satisfy the "liked by all boys in $B$" condition. Wait, the condition is "all girls in the subset are liked by all boys in the subset".
  If we add a girl $g'$, the new subset is $G' = G \cup \{g'\}$ and $B$ remains. For condition 1 to hold in the new subset, we need $\forall b \in B, g'$ likes $b$.
  So, if there exists a girl $g' \notin G$ who likes every boy in $B$, we could add her. T

--- Synthesized Tool Code ---
def solve():
    # Number of boys
    n_boys = 6
    # The maximum number of ideal companies is 2^(number of boys)
    # This is achieved when the sets of girls liked by each boy are chosen
    # such that the intersection of any subset of boys' preferences is unique.
    # Since 9 girls (universe size) is sufficient to distinguish 2^6 = 64 subsets,
    # the maximum number of such closed sets (ideal companies) is 64.
    return str(2 ** n_boys)

