st possible $g$ is $N$ (if $N | 10^k-1$). But we saw $N$ multiple of $99999$ implies $E=1$ which is impossible. \nNext, $g$ could be $N/p$. Then $E = (10^k-1)/(N/p) = p(10^k-1)/N$. \nIf $N=11235$, $10^k-1 = 99999$. $99999/11235 = 8.9$. $g=3$. $E = 99999/3 = 33333$. \nIs there any $N$ containing 11235 such that $N$ divides $10^k-1$ for some $k$? \nIf $N | 10^k-1$, then $E=1$. But we proved $N=10^k-1$ is required if $N < 10^k$ and $N | 10^k-1$? No, $N$ could be a smaller multiple. $N = m(10^k-1)$. Since $N < 10^k$, $m$ must be 0? No $m \\ge 1$. $m(10^k-1) < 10^k \\implies m=1$. So $N=10^k-1$. Impossible. \nSo $E$ cannot be 1. \nWhat about $E=2$? Requires $g = (10^k-1)/2$. Impossible as $10^k-1$ is odd. \n$E=3$? $g = (10^k-1)/3 = R_k$ (repunit of 3s). Need $N$ multiple of $R_k$. And $N < 10^k$. $N = m R_k$. $m R_k < 10^k = 3 R_k$. So $m=1, 2$. \n$m=1: N=33333$. No. \n$m=2: N=66666$. No. \n$E=4$? $g = (10^k-1)/4$. Not integer. \n$E=5$? $g = (10^k-1)/5$. $10^k-1$ not div by 5 ($99..9$ ends in 9). \n$E=6$? $g = (10^k-1)/6$. Not int. \n$E=7$? $g = (10^k-1)/7$. Need $7 | 10^k-1$. Order of 10 mod 7 is 6. So $k$ multiple of 6. \nLet $k=6$. $10^6-1 = 999999$. $g = 142857$. \nNeed $N$ multiple of $142857$. And $N < 10^6$. \nMultiples: $1 \\times 142857 = 142857$ (no 11235). \n$2 \\times = 285714$. \n$3 \\times = 428571$. \n$4 \\times = 571428$. \n$5 \\times = 714285$. \n$6 \\times = 857142$. \nNone contain 11235. \n$E=9$? $g = (10^k-1)/9 = R_k$ (repunit of 1s). \nNeed $N$ multiple of $11111$ etc. \nFor $k=5$, $g=11111$. $N$ multiple of 11111, $N < 100000$. \n$m=1..8$. \n$11111$, $22222$, ..., $133332$ ($>100000$). \nCheck $4 \\times 11111 = 44444$. \n$3 \\times = 33333$. \n$2 \\times = 22222$. \n$1 \\times = 11111$. \nNone contain 11235. \nCheck $k=6$, $g=111111$. Multiples $1..8$. \n$111111$, $222222$, $333333$, ... $777777$. \nNone. \nCheck $k=15$? $10^{15}$ too big. \nMaybe $N$ is not a multiple of the repunit, but the gcd is something else. \nWe found $N=11235, k=5 \to 3333