Consider the parity of the sum of coordinates $S_k = x_k + y_k$.
Each move changes $x$ by $\pm 1$ or $\pm 2$ and $y$ by $\pm 2$ or $\pm 1$.
The change in sum is $\Delta S = \Delta x + \Delta y$.
Possible moves:
$(1, 2) \to \Delta S = 3$
$(1, -2) \to \Delta S = -1$
$(-1, 2) \to \Delta S = 1$
$(-1, -2) \to \Delta S = -3$
$(2, 1) \to \Delta S = 3$
$(2, -1) \to \Delta S = 1$
$(-2, 1) \to \Delta S = -1$
$(-2, -1) \to \Delta S = -3$
In all cases, $\Delta S$ is odd.
Therefore, after each move, the parity of $x_k + y_k$ flips.
Start: $x_0 + y_0 = 2$ (even).
After 1 move: odd.
After 2 moves: even.
...
After $k$ moves: parity is even if $k$ is even, odd if $k$ is odd.
Here, $n = 2009$ is odd. So the final position $(x, y)$ must satisfy $x + y$ being odd.
This means one coordinate is even and the other is odd.
On an $8 \times 8$ board, the squares can be colored like a chessboard (black and white).
