--- Tool Creator Reasoning ---
The problem asks for the sum $S = \sum_{n=1}^{10^6} n f(n)$, where $f(n)$ is the number of integer pairs $(x, y)$ satisfying $x^2 + xy + y^2 = n$.
Substituting $f(n)$ into the sum, we get:
$S = \sum_{n=1}^{N} n \sum_{\substack{x, y \in \mathbb{Z} \\ x^2 + xy + y^2 = n}} 1$
where $N = 10^6$.
We can interchange the order of summation. Instead of summing over $n$ and then finding pairs for that $n$, we sum over all integer pairs $(x, y)$ and add the value of the expression $x^2 + xy + y^2$ which corresponds to the $n$.
Let $Q(x, y) = x^2 + xy + y^2$. Then $n = Q(x, y)$.
The condition $1 \le n \le N$ means $1 \le Q(x, y) \le N$.
Since $Q(x, y)$ represents a positive definite quadratic form, $Q(x, y) = 0$ if and only if $x=y=0$. Thus, for any $(x, y) \neq (0, 0)$, $Q(x, y) \ge 1$.
